Java Binding: The "Remote vs. Device" Rule
In Java, the way the language finds a method or a variable depends on whether it is "Behavior" or "State."
1. Methods (Behavior)
 * When: Resolved at Run-time (Dynamic Binding).
 * Rule: Java looks at the Actual Object (the "Device") created with the new keyword.
 * Key Concept: Method Overriding. If a child class has its own version, that one runs.
 * Analogy: You press the "Play" button on a remote. If the device is a DVD player, it plays a disc. If it's a Streamer, it plays a video.
2. Variables (State)
 * When: Resolved at Compile-time (Static Binding).
 * Rule: Java looks at the Reference Type (the "Remote Control" label/type).
 * Key Concept: Variable Hiding. Variables do not support polymorphism.
 * Analogy: If your remote is labeled "Standard," it only sees the buttons (variables) defined for a standard remote, even if it's pointing at a "Pro" TV.
3. The "Static" Exception
 * Static Methods: Like variables, these are resolved at Compile-time based on the Reference Type.
 * Why? Static members belong to the Class, not the object instance.
Summary Table
| Feature | Binding Time | Look at... | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| Instance Method | Run-time | Actual Object | Overriding |
| Variable | Compile-time | Reference Type | Hiding |
| Static Method | Compile-time | Reference Type | Hiding |
